WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Authentication is the process of proving or verifying an identity.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.960
In a web or API application, that identity may be a human user, a service account, a device, or another workload.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:25.340
Passwords remain common, but secure password authentication requires mature handling: strong hashing, rate limiting, account recovery controls, abuse detection, and protection against credential stuffing.

4
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:32.640
Building all of that correctly is difficult, which is why developers should avoid custom authentication when mature identity providers are available.

5
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.560
Multi-factor authentication raises confidence by requiring more than one kind of proof.

6
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:46.380
A password plus a one-time code, push approval, hardware key, or passkey is stronger than a password alone.

7
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:54.560
Passkeys, at a high level, use public key cryptography to reduce reliance on shared secrets and improve phishing resistance.

8
00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:08.660
The implementation details are usually handled by platforms and identity providers, but developers still need to understand how MFA and passkeys affect enrollment, recovery, session behavior, and user experience.

9
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.780
Federated login and single sign-on let applications rely on an external identity provider.

10
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:21.680
That can reduce password handling risk and improve central control, but it does not remove application responsibility.

11
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:31.460
Developers must integrate the provider correctly, validate responses, handle account linking carefully, and decide how identity attributes map to local users and permissions.

12
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:38.120
Account recovery is especially sensitive because it can bypass the normal login path.

13
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:45.020
Treat recovery as an authentication flow with the same security attention as the primary sign-in experience.
